<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE electron-hawkeye360 ascent (3DOF sim)</name><description>ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Electron &#x27;We&#x27;re Not Hawking Around&#x27; (3x HawkEye 360) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Method: first-principles 3DOF sim (/workspace/trajectory-deck/sim/ascent3dof.py): WGS-84 rotating Earth, J2, US76 drag,
thrust SL/vac interpolated on ambient pressure, mdot from Isp, staging/fairing/coast events; vertical rise to 8.0 s (rule: config guidance),
pitch kick 3.710 deg over 10 s, gravity turn, linear-tangent upper-stage steering tan(theta)=0.6257-0.001676*(t-t_ign), solved by optimizer.
Vehicle config: Rocket Lab Electron (sim/vehicles/electron.json; every number carries a source URL, derivation, or ASSUMPTION).
Electron: GLOW, S2 propellant, thrusts, Isp, fairing mass/time from Rocket Lab Electron Payload User Guide (7.0 content, 8.0 URL).
DERIVED: S1 propellant (0.90 fraction ASSUMPTION), effective mdot from published burn times; ASSUMED S2 dry 250 kg, Cd 0.4, 8 s vertical rise.
Target (see launch config): insertion 230 km, apoapsis 520 km, fpa 0, launch azimuth 189.06 deg; payload 180 kg.
Pad: -39.262833N 177.864469E. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s (Earth-relative).
T0 used: 2026-10-23T07:11:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.
POST-CUTOFF: deploy time unpublished (Rocket Lab publishes no deploy or Curie burn time (third-party log: separation ~08:04Z), and this untuned energy-mode ascent ends in a ~216 x 590 km (osculating) orbit vs Rocket Lab&#x27;s 520 km, so a Kick Stage coast would not match sources; deferred until Electron is re-tuned). From insertion T+534.5 s the stage is propagated for TWO revolutions (osculating period 92.63 min) on the inferred insertion orbit (same WGS-84/J2/US76-drag model); rows tagged -coast.
POST-CUTOFF end T+11650 s: inferred insertion orbit, +2 revs (deploy time unpublished): alt 229.49 km, osculating 216.1 x 591.3 km, i 95.806 deg.</description>
